WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Cat fashion,
photons, and load balancers.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.150
How do they all relate?

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.600
YURIY BABENKO: Stay
tuned to learn more.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.597 --> 00:00:14.180
STEPHANIE WONG: For many people,
one of the greatest mysteries

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.800
in the universe is
how in the world

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.380
Google can return billions
of cat image results

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.770
in under one second.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.530
So I brought my
friend Yuriy here

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.095
to help explain
just how we do that.

00:00:23.095 --> 00:00:24.470
YURIY BABENKO:
While the internet

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.050
has a whole set of
optimizations to make things

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.930
like these searches fast,
Google's global architecture

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.200
has its own secret sauce that
allows it to achieve the speed

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.360
and accuracy that
you've come to know,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.000
which prompts many customers
and companies to ask

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.120
how to leverage that
performance for their own needs.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.520
STEPHANIE WONG: Well, Google
spent the last two decades

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.280
building one of the largest,
fastest fiber optic networks

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.470
in the world to
support Google Cloud

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:52.040
and Google's own services-- you
know, like YouTube and Gmail.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.340
And that network has grown 15
times in the past six years,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.530
passing 600 trillion
bits per second

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.500
across land and through sea,
allowing us to service over

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.043
1 billion users a day.

00:01:02.043 --> 00:01:03.710
YURIY BABENKO: In
building this network,

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.430
Google has had to put in years
of research, some of which

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.430
has made it back into the
community as white papers,

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.590
which talk about more of
their amazing achievements

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:13.262
in technology.

00:01:13.262 --> 00:01:15.470
STEPHANIE WONG: But we're
getting ahead of ourselves.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.810
To understand how big
this technology is,

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.703
let's start by getting
small and looking

00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:21.620
at how the internet works
from the perspective

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.050
of a single photon running
through Google's network.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.600
Let's say we just created our
latest web e-commerce site

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.840
on Google Cloud that serves
the latest cat fashion trends.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.720
We have some users in Singapore
who are testing our new site

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.400
and send a request
from their device

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.108
to purchase cat winter apparel.

00:01:36.108 --> 00:01:38.150
YURIY BABENKO: The request
from the user's device

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.230
will hit the service
provider first,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.460
which will realize the
destination is a Google server.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.610
It will send it right to the
closest Google front-end server

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:47.980
that it can find.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.810
These GFEs sit at the very edge
of Google's global network,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.330
with multiple
points of presence.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.080
Why?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.960
To allow information to be
served as close to the users

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.652
as possible, like Rio de
Janeiro, Zurich, and Singapore.

00:02:00.652 --> 00:02:03.110
STEPHANIE WONG: At this point,
Google's technological magic

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.040
takes over.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.570
The first thing
the request does is

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.060
slam into Google Cloud's
software-defined global load

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:08.970
balancer.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.380
This load balancer is
responsible for distributing

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.020
HTTP and HTTP(S) traffic
to back-ends in order

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.870
to manage and distribute
load in a scalable way.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.660
In the best scenario, we've
deployed our software onto VMs

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.000
running in the Singapore
data center, in which case

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.542
the request is handled and the
data is sent back to the user.

00:02:25.542 --> 00:02:27.958
YURIY BABENKO: But let's say
the closest back-end instance

00:02:27.958 --> 00:02:29.840
group in Singapore
is unavailable,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.020
or maybe we haven't deployed
VMs to this region yet.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.270
The user's request is
seamlessly directed

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.430
to other VMs we are
running stationed

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.990
in the US-East region.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.990
What is great about the
global L7 load balancer

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.900
is that routing to a different
region across the globe

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.820
happens without the developer
having to lift a finger.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.780
And configuration is just
a few clicks in the UI.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.620
It gives you a single Anycast IP
that you can point traffic to,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.448
even if the back-ends go down.

00:02:56.448 --> 00:02:58.490
STEPHANIE WONG: What lets
the L7 load balancer do

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.470
this is the fact that
all of the load balancing

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.322
is done in software,
rather than in hardware.

00:03:03.322 --> 00:03:05.780
It performs a weighted selection
from the set of back-ends,

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.090
skipping any back-ends
that appear unhealthy

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.170
or whose connections
are saturated.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.763
This is called the waterfall
by region algorithm,

00:03:12.763 --> 00:03:14.180
and we'll have an
in-depth episode

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.790
on this on our other series,
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.160
Traffic is proxied
through these GFEs, which

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.140
forwards the query to
the selected back-end

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.160
running our cat web server.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.580
YURIY BABENKO: Now, in our
scenario of a user in Singapore

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.400
requesting data from
a VM in US-East,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.390
the GFEs send the request across
a subsea fiber optical cable

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.880
from APAC to the
US, which connects

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.400
various operational
regions of Google Cloud.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.650
These fiber networks
connect Cloud regions

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.410
across land with
thousands of kilometers.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.350
You've probably passed them
and never even noticed.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.660
They are underground, along
railroad tracks, across

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.750
[INAUDIBLE],, or even
across mountain ranges.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.720
And if you're wondering
about the speed,

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.350
photons travel at [INAUDIBLE]
of the speed of light in fiber.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.450
And with that speed,
our photons can

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.380
carry over 2,000,000 photos
and 8,000 YouTube videos

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.257
every second across our network.

00:04:05.257 --> 00:04:07.340
STEPHANIE WONG: Bear with
me on what happens next.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.440
That Anycast IP the
L7 load balancer

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.390
has comes with an
associated forwarding

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:14.270
rule, which will then direct
traffic to a target proxy.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.240
Here, the target proxy
terminates the client session.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.459
It has configurations
for a URL map,

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.220
like host and path rules,
which then routes the traffic

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.162
to the correct back-end service
on VMs in the US-East region.

00:04:26.162 --> 00:04:27.620
YURIY BABENKO: The
back-end servers

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:30.350
check each back-end's
capacity and health

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.535
and sends traffic to the most
available and healthy back-end.

00:04:33.535 --> 00:04:36.170
It then sends the
shopping data back.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.635
The GFE caches the response
and forwards it to the user.

00:04:39.635 --> 00:04:41.250
STEPHANIE WONG: And
this, my friends,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.360
is how you can optimize for
latency by choosing from Google

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.430
Cloud's globally
extensive regions

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.560
that are closest
to your users, even

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.840
when back-ends are
overloaded or unhealthy.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.900
Or you can use Google Cloud
content delivery network

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.730
to leverage our
load-balancing and cache

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.323
content closer to your users.

00:04:57.323 --> 00:04:59.240
YURIY BABENKO: But let's
go back for a second.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.310
We would be remiss
if we didn't discuss

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.740
in a bit more
detail what happens

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.230
as the content is sent back to
the user from the back-ends.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.850
As the response is served
back to the GFE from the VM,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.010
photons are sent
from our servers,

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.680
which are connected to optical
transmission equipment.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.530
These use infrared lasers
to transmit signals

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:19.960
through the fiber network.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.390
A typical cable would have
hundreds of optical fibers,

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:27.590
each made of a glass, with a
diameter of just a human hair.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.010
And they can transmit signals
with terabytes capacity.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.100
Then the photons leave the
Google data center's fibers

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.710
that are bundled in an
outside plant cable.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.170
STEPHANIE WONG: But we
inevitably face a problem.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.780
As photons travel down
fiber optic cables,

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.815
their intensity diminishes,
called attenuation.

00:05:44.815 --> 00:05:47.420
For example, in one of
Google's research papers,

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.070
they demonstrated what happens
when optical signals travel

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.250
through 3,200 kilometers.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.770
Looking at the graph, you can
see that the relative power

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.600
level of the signal decreased.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.790
So how do photons
make it all the way

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.358
between regions which are
thousands of kilometers apart?

00:06:01.358 --> 00:06:02.900
YURIY BABENKO: To
combat attenuation,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.260
we place optical amplifiers
along our cables,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.450
with hundreds of
them on the route.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.580
Amplifiers increase a
signal over long distances,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.972
so our photon on can
make it to a destination.

00:06:13.972 --> 00:06:15.680
STEPHANIE WONG: The
journey of the photon

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.670
finally ends back in Singapore.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:20.360
It traveled along fiber
laid underground and strung

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.010
through the air,
getting amplified

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.430
along the way, all of which
happen in just milliseconds.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.800
Our fiber networks now connect
to Google Cloud regions

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.690
across five continents.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.670
Over the past 10 years,
through a combination

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.250
of buying and leasing, Google
has fiber all around the world.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.770
But this is only the beginning.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.270
There is so much
more in development

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.815
to support our
future cloud regions.

00:06:39.815 --> 00:06:41.690
YURIY BABENKO: In fact,
you can automatically

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.330
take advantage of these
by using the Premium Tier

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.990
network to access Google's
own global private network,

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.330
avoiding suboptimal
internet path.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.137
Check out the link
below to learn more.

00:06:52.137 --> 00:06:53.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Like
this video if you

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.500
want more content like this.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.320
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.660
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

